{{Quote|I was happy that they took me in. My parents were killed and the Mandalorians took care of me.|Din Djarin, to [[Omera]]|<ref name="Chapter 4">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 4: Sanctuary}}</ref>}}
A [[Gender|male]] [[Mandalorian]] '''warrior''' served as part of the [[Death Watch]] group during the [[time]] of the [[Clone Wars]]. During a [[Confederacy of Independent Systems|Separatist Alliance]] [[Attack on Aq Vetina|attack]] on the [[planet]] of [[Aq Vetina]], the warrior rescued the young [[Youngling|child]] [[Din Djarin]] from a [[B2-series super battle droid]]. As Djarin's [[parent]]s had [[Death|died]] while attempting to keep him hidden, the warrior flew out of the battle with the boy, taking him in as a [[foundling]].

==Biography==
{{Quote|I was a foundling.|Din Djarin|<ref name="Chapter 8" />}}
During the [[Clone Wars]],<ref name="Chapter 8" /> a [[The galaxy|galactic]]-wide conflict between the [[Galactic Republic]] and the [[Confederacy of Independent Systems]],<ref name="EPII">{{Film|II}}</ref> a [[Mandalorian warrior]]<ref name="Chapter 8" /> was a [[soldier]]<ref name="Novel" /> of the [[Death Watch]],<ref name="Chapter 8" /> a [[Mandalorian]] splinter group which initially served as opposition to the pacifist [[New Mandalorians|government]] of the [[planet]] [[Mandalore]] before facing [[Mandalorian Civil War (19 BBY)|internal strike]].<ref name="Eminence">{{TCW|Eminence (episode)}}</ref> For its planned coup, Death Watch allied with the Confederacy,<ref name="DOM">{{TCW|Duchess of Mandalore}}</ref> but relations eventually broke down between both groups, turning Death Watch into an enemy of the Separatist Alliance.<ref name="A Friend in Need">{{TCW|A Friend in Need}}</ref> In [[20 BBY]] or [[19 BBY]],<ref name="Date">As noted here [[Template:OnderonianCivilWarDate|here]], the events of the ''[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars]]'' episode "[[Tipping Points]]" occur in [[20 BBY]]. {{BuildR2Cite|45|Droid Directory}} states that the [[HMP droid gunship]] was first brought into action at the [[Onderonian Civil War]], a deployment depicted in "Tipping Points". ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian]]'' episode "[[Chapter 8: Redemption]]" shows that the attack on Aq Vetina took place during the [[time]] of the [[Clone Wars]]. As ''Star Wars: Galactic Atlas'' states the war ended in 19 [['ABY-BBY' dating system|BBY]], and the attack features HMP [[droid]] [[gunship]]s, it must take place in 20 BBY or 19 BBY.</ref> Separatist<ref name="Guide to Season One">[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian: Guide to Season One|''Star Wars: The Mandalorian'': Guide to Season One]]</ref> forces [[Attack on Aq Vetina|attacked]] a [[human]] settlement<ref name="Chapter 8" /> the [[planet]]<ref name="Encyclopedia">{{EncyclopediaCite|Din Djarin and Other Characters of the Underworld}}</ref> [[Aq Vetina]], [[Death|killing]] many of the residents. However, members of the Death Watch, including the Mandalorian warrior, arrived to fend off the Separatist [[B2-series super battle droid]]s. The soldier gunned down a [[battle droid]] which was attempting to kill the young boy [[Din Djarin]], who was hidden in a bunker.<ref name="Chapter 8" />

[[File:Death_Watch_rescue.png|thumb|left|300px|The Mandalorian warrior exits the battle with the orphan Din Djarin]]
Djarin's [[parent]]s had been killed after hiding the boy, and the warrior beckoned Djarin and helped the boy out of the bunker while other members of the Death Watch landed and gunned down super battle [[droid]]s. The pair watched the fighting before<ref name="Chapter 8" /> one of the other Mandalorians gestured the soldier to lift off<ref name="Novel" /> with Djarin. The Mandalorian warrior complied, and picked up the boy with his consent, flying up from the settlement with the orphan by [[jetpack]]. Djarin became a Mandalorian [[foundling]], and swore to the [[Mandalorian Creed]] when he was of age. He later reminisced his rescue by the Mandalorian warrior while pinned down in a [[Nevarro school|common house]] on the planet [[Nevarro]] by [[Gideon's battalion|forces]] of a [[Galactic Empire]] [[Gideon's Imperial remnant|remnant]]<ref name="Chapter 8" /> in [[9 ABY]].<ref name="9 BBY">{{MandoDate|8}}</ref>

==Equipment==
[[File:Sideshow-Mandopointsblaster.png|thumb|right|300px|The Mandalorian wielded a blaster and wore blue armor.]]
The Mandalorian warrior wore [[Color|blue]] [[Mandalorian armor]] like other members of the Death Watch movement. The warrior's armor had the Death Watch symbol<ref name="Chapter 8" />&mdash;the image of the [[Shriek-hawk]]<ref name="AEYNTK">''[[Star Wars: Absolutely Everything You Need to Know]]''</ref>&mdash;on its left shoulder pad, while the right pad had three white stripes, which were also seen on the soldier's chest plate and right thigh plate.<ref name="Sixth Scale">{{SideshowCite|set=Sixth Scale Figures|pack=Death Watch Mandalorian|link=collectibles/star-wars-death-watch-mandalorian-hot-toys-907141}}</ref> The soldier also donned brown [[clothing]] under the [[armor]]. Beyond the armor, the warrior was equipped with [[Unidentified Mandalorian blaster rifle|a blaster rifle]], a jetpack, a [[rangefinder]], and [[Mandalorian vambrace]]s.<ref name="Chapter 8" /> Additionally, the warrior owned a [[WESTAR-35 blaster pistol]].<ref name="Sixth Scale" />

==Behind the scenes==
{{Quote|I thought it would be really fun to make the Mandalorian that rescued him the Ralph McQuarrie white version. If this guardian in white reaches down for you, you're going to go with him.|Christian Alzmann, on drawing the Mandalorian warrior|<ref name="Art of Mando">[[The Art of Star Wars: The Mandalorian (Season One)|''The Art of Star Wars: The Mandalorian'' Season One]]</ref>}}
[[File:Brendan-Aiden-Favreau-DGM.png|thumb|left|300px|Aiden Bertola, the actor of young Din Djarin, tries on Brenden Wayne's costume helmet.]]
The Mandalorian warrior first appeared in a flashback sequence in "[[Chapter 8: Redemption]]"<ref name="Chapter 8" /> of the [[The Mandalorian Season One|first season]] of the [[Disney+]] series ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian]]''. The episode was directed by [[Taika Waititi]] and released on [[December 27]], [[2019]].<ref name="S1 Companion">{{InsiderCite|201|Star Wars: The Mandalorian Season One Companion|''Star Wars: The Mandalorian'' Season One Companion}}</ref> Credited as "Mandalorian Warrior," the character was played by [[Brendan Wayne]], who also acted as a body double for Din Djarin, the show's protagonist.<ref name="Chapter 8" />

Concept art for the soldier was conceived by artist [[Christian Alzmann]] among at least ten versions on the scene itself. In version 7, the artist based the warrior off of the white Mandalorian concept art drawn by [[Ralph McQuarrie]]<ref name="Art of Mando" /> which became groundwork for [[Boba Fett]].<ref name="Art of Ralph">''[[The Art of Ralph McQuarrie]]''</ref> Two other versions the Mandalorian warrior and his Death Watch comrades were made to look biblical and dreamy to represent the flashback nature of the scene.<ref name="Art of Mando" />

